  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nDownstream of an amplified upper ridge over West Coast, an upper low \nis dropping sharply southeast out of Canada into the northern High \nPlains. Over the next few days, this upper low will phase with a \nsouthern stream shortwave and amplify to our east. While too far \neast for any notable snowfall in our area, the upper low will \nreinforce the current chilly pattern for a few more days. Highs \nthrough Thanksgiving day will stay in the upper 30s to low 40s, with \nlows in the low 20s through Friday morning. As mentioned above, the \nupper low will amplify a bit too far to our east to bring much \nprecipitation to our area. That said, we will have a couple \nopportunity for a few conversational snowflakes - i.e. no \naccumulation or impacts, but just enough to look out the window and \nsay \"hey, it's snowing\". The first chance will be Wednesday morning \nacross portions of northern KS as the upper low initially approaches \nthe area, while the second will be mid-day Thursday across far \neastern KS as a weakening TROWAL wobbles back to the west.\n\nTemperatures warm back to near average Friday and Saturday as \nshortwave ridging builds in behind the upper low and ahead of another\ndeepening shortwave. Guidance differs in the amplitude of this next \nshortwave. The GFS still has a shallower wave with only some brief \nlight rain, while deeper solutions have a more notable band of \nprecipitation moving across northern Kansas. Expecting only rain at \nthe moment given a lack of colder air, though can't completely rule \nout some wet snow if a deeper trough with a stronger surface low can \nverify. Beyond the weekend, the up and down pattern with near average\ntemps looks to continue. Good agreement in another progressive \nshortwave trough approaching sometime Monday into Tuesday.",
